00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the stress of the coronavirus pandemic  is taking its toll on all of us  there's the ever-present fear that you  or a loved one  could catch the virus  on top of the strain of having to work  and learn  from home with normal ways of  relaxing and socializing on hold for now  scientists are starting to study the  emotional impact the pandemic is having  on people everywhere from the oldest  to the youngest  is  [Music]  [Music]  children perhaps can't fully comprehend  the unusual strain that this pandemic is  placing upon them  these are not normal times for anyone  and the impact is being felt strongly by  those  who would normally need the most help  the elderly are among them  three friends have met up to play cards  in leipzig germany  they are between 70 and well over 90  years old  they all still live in their own homes  they regularly do exercise classes  together  and meet up in a restaurant on sundays  so how do they feel about the contact  restrictions  i don't want to take any risks i prefer  to follow the recommendations  i won't take any risks  of course this is stressful because you  become really isolated  and very alone and you need a bit more  at least i personally need a little bit  more company  nursing homes have had restrictions for  a long time  visits are now limited to half an hour  each day  one of this home's residents is lucia  litman she is 99 years old  has four children and she's a grand and  great grandmother  dimensions  humans are meant to be social you can  have a good life when you have closeness  when you can hug things that aren't  possible now  i don't dare to shake hands with the  children that's not what you're supposed  to do  and that's the hardest part  litman has experienced a lot in her life  escape from war  illnesses and great upheavals there  could not be a lesson  all we can do is stay calm and accept  whatever comes our way  the only thing we can influence is by  being considerate to each other  the impact of the coronavirus on the  mental health of people of  all ages is something that's being  studied very closely  we can now speak to professor christoph  carrell from the charity medical school  here in berlin  he's conducting a major international  survey  called the collaborative outcomes study  on health  and functioning during infection times  or  co-fit for short and i think we need a  short version of that thank you very  much for joining us professor i mean  what's the aim  of your study yes when kofit  actually covet hit the world we were  stunned by it  myself and marco solmi the co-pi and we  thought we need to do something  to learn from it and feel fortified so  the idea is to  stay and be fit co-fit during the covet  pandemic and other pandemics trying to  understand  who are the people who are at highest  risk of having poor outcomes  from it and what are coping strategies  to actually  do well during the pandemic so that we  could learn from in the from individuals  for  individuals but also to do something  like evidence-based governance  that politicians and society could learn  from  what kind of strategies to deal with the  pandemic  help both physical and mental well-being  or actually make it worse so what sort  of thing are you asking people  so we're asking many things it's a long  survey that's a little bit of  a weakness about 30 minutes for adults  and adolescents in about 15 to 20  minutes  for children age 6 to 13 we're asking  them obviously where they live how they  live  whether they know someone who has been  infected or they've been infected  themselves  if they're in quarantine right now or  not but also do they have a physical or  mental illness already  and what is their help seeking behavior  during the pandemic are they getting the  medication or the care they need  and then we ask symptoms looking at not  full validated scales but we drew out  single questions  to be as trans diagnostic as possible  having as many  mental domains and physical domains we  want to cover  and then we already saw that having  drawn out just a couple of questions  always on zero to ten  how much do you feel it is true now that  you're anxious  lonely bored stressed  angry and how was it the two weeks  before the pandemic hit  in order to see a change and then we  also ask about coping strategies what  works the best  and then we want to see people who do  well what kind of coping strategies do  they use  versus those that don't so that we can  actually  recommend certain strategies to people  who may not use them appropriately  there are already certain theories on  the impact that the pandemic's been  having on mental health are there  specific um ideas that you're trying to  either challenge or  or prove with this survey  so i mean we are looking both at  non-modifiable risk factors which  are basically subgroup definitions  and modifiable risk factors so can we do  something  that we want to change so for example um  how much people go walk outside how much  they  are in contact with others is it just  personal contact or what about  also online contact that is already  pretty good  what about telemedicine that supplements  for in-person visits and can still  maintain  well-being so we're we're testing a  couple of  also resilience factors does resilience  change over time or is it something that  you are  born with into the pandemic what about  altruism  can altruistic behavior change and  thereby also improve  outcomes these are all some of the  questions we're asking  we've been looking on the program at uh  specifically the impact for young people  and older people are there specific  groups that you are expecting to see  the pandemic having a larger impact on  yeah so we're looking at first of all  it's a it's an anonymous survey for the  general population but we're very  interested in subgroups  migrants pregnant women women in general  the young the very old the people who  live alone  frontline healthcare workers all of  these will be examined we currently have  a hundred and ten thousand people across  the world  147 countries six continents to  participate we need more people to  understand it better but what we've  learned already is that  women seem to bear the brunt they have  more stress they're more angry and they  also  feel somewhat more lonely than males  and particularly it seems those that  have to do  triple tasking they're at home running  the household  but they have to do homeschooling and  maybe also do  home office kind of work and it seems  that they are  much more affected or they're more open  and honest about it  because also more women participated in  the survey so far  it will be interesting to see which  groups do turn out to be the greater  effective professor christoph carrell  from the charity medical school  and the co-fit study thanks for joining  us  thanks for having me and you can take  part in this survey  by visiting cofit.com they want people  from all over  the world and it's available in lots of  different languages and you can help  create a better understanding of the  human impact of covet 19.  now is the part of the program where we  put one of your questions submitted  through  our youtube channel to our science  correspondent derek williams  i would like to know why the pfizer  vaccine needs to be kept so very cold  especially since it presumably has to be  warmed up prior to administration  to answer this i first have to go over a  few basics of cell biology  the vaccine developed by biontec and  pfizer  is what's known as a messenger rna or  mrna  vaccine now mrna is a chain like  molecule that fulfills a very important  function in cells it contains  codes to make proteins and it acts  as a messenger between a cell's control  center and  and its protein-building machinery as  hence the name  but the protein this particular mrna  vaccine encodes for  um isn't a human one it's it's a protein  that's made  by the coronavirus and when the mrna is  injected  it causes your cells to begin making  that viral protein and that provokes  an immune response just as if you caught  covet 19.  sending in the pretty simple quick to  produce  mrna code molecules to make these these  complicated proteins  and getting the body to do all of the  work of producing them  is an elegant solution to to a complex  problem  but of course it can't be all easy um  we've been trying to produce  these kinds of vaccines for for decades  now and and one of the major hurdles  has been keeping the mrna stable  long enough for it to accomplish its  task  after all messenger rna isn't built to  last and the cell  it's supposed to break down again after  its proteins have been produced not hang  around because  that would cause all kinds of problems  so lots of the research in the field  involves keeping this pretty  fragile molecule stable and  and one way to do that is to coat it in  a specially designed  molecular envelope and then and then  freeze it  at ultra low temperatures when you  thought back out again  the the mrna begins to degrade but but  not instantly um  the best description i've heard of the  process compared these vaccines to to  chocolate covered ice cream bars with  with the coating  helping to retain the integrity of the  ice cream to some extent  even as it slowly melts  pfizer and biontech say that their  vaccine if refrigerated  remains stable for for up to to about  five days after thawing and  moderna which which makes the other mrna  vaccine candidate that's making  headlines at the moment  it says that its doses have a  refrigerated shelf life of up to 30 days  post it as a comment on our youtube  channel that's all from us  stay healthy

